{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794396538,"nonce":"0GeomqcL/t8J18d3dGGkfAyqBbU=","message":"Lent callbacks work on every runtime: a Haxe function passed to a native call, called during that call on the calling thread.\n\nHow an exception is kept out of native code. The binding wraps the user's function: the wrapper catches, hands the exception to `hxn.Callback.failed`, and returns null. The trampoline turns null into `Status::CALLBACK`, the library stops and returns that failure, and `hxn.Callback.during`, which the binding made the native call inside, throws the function's own exception. Nothing is thrown through a Rust frame, so this needs no trap in native code and is the same on HashLink, hxcpp and both wasm builds.\n\nPieces:\n\n- hxn-abi: `Status::CALLBACK`, and `Callback::invoke` for the conventional shape (returns a status, writes its result through a last `out`).\n- hxn-hl: `call` over `hl_dyn_call`, with `box_i32` and `unbox_i32`. `hl_dyn_call_safe` is not used: Ash's libhl does not export it, and the wrapper makes it unnecessary.\n- hxcpp: the trampoline is C++ glue that calls the `Dynamic`.\n- Haxe: `hxn.Callback` holds the pending exception per thread.\n\nTested through the fixture's `Counter.apply` on Ash (hybrid, jit and interp), Ash wasm, stock HashLink, hxcpp and Emscripten: called ten times; an exception on the third call reaches the caller and leaves the value as the second call left it; a callback that calls back into the same counter gets \"handle is in use\" and the counter is free afterwards.\n\nNot done:\n\n- Stored callbacks, kept past the call. A `Callback` is not `Send`, so it cannot go in a `Handle\u003cT\u003e`, and its root has to be removed on the Haxe thread, not from a finalizer inside a collection. That needs a holder tied to a thread and a deferred release.\n- Argument and result types beyond `Int`. Each needs its box on HashLink (`hlt_f64`, `hlt_bool`, bytes) and its conversion in the C++ glue.\n- Calling a Haxe function from a task. It is reachable now only for work stepped on the Haxe thread, which is the single-thread queue; on the workers it would be a foreign thread.\n\nFound on the way, and fixed in `hxn.Future.await`: in Ash's `--mode interp` a thread made by `Thread.create` runs until it first sleeps and then resumes only while another thread is in `Sys.sleep` with a positive duration. A native call that blocks does not let it in, so `await` blocking in the library never saw a future that such a thread settles. `await` now waits in 20 ms slices with a 1 ms sleep between. That looks like Ash's cooperative threading rather than a bug, so it was not reported to the Ash session.","files":null}]}